-- outbound.eod — the same road as `drift.eod`, forty miles an hour faster.
--
-- Upbeat without being busy, which are two different axes and get confused
-- constantly. `techno.eod` is busy because sixteen things happen per bar;
-- `synthwave.eod` is busy because its arpeggio runs at sixteenths under a
-- lead. This piece runs at 140 with a straight eighth-note bass and an
-- offbeat hat — a fast *pulse* — while the harmony still moves once every two
-- bars and the melody is four notes in seven. Pulse is what makes it drive.
-- Event count per bar is what makes it tiring. Only the first is wanted here.
--
-- The feel is half-time: the backbeat lands every two beats rather than every
-- one, so 140 is counted as 70 by anyone tapping along. That is the trick the
-- genre uses to be quick and unhurried at once.
--
---------------------------------------------------------------------- form
--
-- Form here works on two timescales, and it needs both. The first version of
-- this file had only the second, and the result was a piece that never
-- repeated and also never went anywhere: the same nine instruments played all
-- the time, so nothing could arrive.
--
-- **Sections, 32 bars.** Each layer carries a `blocks` pattern of eight
-- four-bar steps that scales its velocity. That is an ordinary pattern rather
-- than an arrangement curve, so it does not run out — it is the difference
-- between `line(0, 1, bars(8))` and something the piece can be left playing.
-- The eight steps are staggered per layer, so instruments enter and leave:
--
--            bars    1-4  5-8  9-12 13-16 17-20 21-24 25-28 29-32
--   bass             on   on   on   on    on    on    on    on
--   kick             on   on   on   on    on    on    --    on
--   snare            --   on   on   on    on    on    --    on
--   hats             --   --   on   on    on    on    --    on
--   stabs            on   on   low  low   on    on    on    low
--   mallet           --   on   on   --    --    on    on    on
--   lead             --   --   --   on    on    --    --    on
--   wash (sustain)   on   on   --   --    on    --    on    --
--
--   bell             --   --   --   --    on    on    on    on    (fader at 0)
--
-- Bar 25 is the one thing in the piece that could be called a drop: the kit
-- leaves for four bars and the bass, stabs, mallet and pad carry it alone.
--
-- Every part also has a live fader — see the desk below. Which layer is the
-- distracting one is a listening judgement, and the file should not be the
-- place that judgement is made.
--
-- **Phase, 3 to 8 bars.** Underneath that, each layer's own loop is a
-- different length, so no two passes of the 32-bar form contain the same
-- detail:
--
--   harmony (stabs, bass, wash)   8 bars    the spine — these stay locked
--   kick, backbeat                4 bars
--   hats                          3 bars rhythm, 7 bars of tone over it
--   mallet                        5 bars, offset 2
--   lead, perc accent             7 bars, offset 3
--   filter sweeps                11 bars
--   level and drive              13 bars
--
-- The notes repeat at lcm(32, 3, 5, 7) = 3360 bars, or about an hour and a
-- half. The 11-bar filter sweeps push that to 17½ hours and the 13-bar level
-- sweeps to 480480 bars, which is nine and a half days. Every bar of it is
-- deterministic and identical on every replay: the pattern algebra derives
-- from cycle position, so a loop of length 5 queried at cycle 4619 answers
-- directly, without anything having had to count its way there.
--
-- One layer has to stay still or the result is soup rather than form, and
-- here that is the bass. Everything else is heard as early or late against
-- it.
--
-- The one real constraint this imposes is harmonic. A 5-bar melodic cell
-- against an 8-bar chord loop means every note lands over a different chord
-- each pass, so the cell has to be consonant with all four. Both cells are
-- drawn from the C# minor pentatonic (c# e f# g# b), which contains no note
-- that is a semitone from a chord tone of C#m, A, E or B. That is also the
-- musical payoff rather than merely the safety condition: the same six notes
-- arrive as a ninth, then a sixth, then a root, and the ear hears three
-- melodies where one was written.

tempo(140)

---------------------------------------------------------------------- space

-- Big and bright, unlike `drift.eod`'s. This one is supposed to be the size
-- of the windscreen.
hall = send { graph = reverb(52, secs(3.4), 0.42) >> highpass(180), level = 0.26 }

-- A quarter-note delay rather than the dotted eighth. At 140 the dotted
-- eighth fills every gap there is, which is precisely the busyness being
-- avoided; the quarter lands on the beat and reinforces the pulse instead of
-- crowding it.
echo = send {
  graph = delay(beats(1))
       >> lowpass(2600)
       >> feedback(0.34),
  level = 0.2,
}

---------------------------------------------------------------------- desk

-- One fader per part, because which layer is "the distracting one" is a
-- listening judgement and guessing at it in the source costs a round trip
-- each time. A `control` is program-scope and writable while the program
-- runs, so these are a mixing desk rather than a set of constants.
--
-- Each one multiplies into its voice's own output gain, beside `n.velocity`,
-- so a fader scales a part *within* its arrangement rather than overriding
-- the section gates. Zero is off; `bell` starts there.
--
-- It has to be inside the graph. A control is a graph-rate value and pattern
-- arithmetic will not take one — which is correct, since a fader that could
-- be written into a pattern would be a fader the scheduler had to re-query.
lvl_bass   = control { name = "bass",   range = { 0, 1.5 }, default = 1 }
lvl_stabs  = control { name = "stabs",  range = { 0, 1.5 }, default = 1 }
lvl_pad    = control { name = "pad",    range = { 0, 1.5 }, default = 1 }
lvl_mallet = control { name = "mallet", range = { 0, 1.5 }, default = 1 }
lvl_lead   = control { name = "lead",   range = { 0, 1.5 }, default = 1 }
lvl_kick   = control { name = "kick",   range = { 0, 1.5 }, default = 1 }
lvl_snare  = control { name = "snare",  range = { 0, 1.5 }, default = 1 }
lvl_hats   = control { name = "hats",   range = { 0, 1.5 }, default = 1 }
lvl_tom    = control { name = "tom",    range = { 0, 1.5 }, default = 1 }
lvl_bell   = control { name = "bell",   range = { 0, 1.5 }, default = 0 }

---------------------------------------------------------------------- rack

-- Six saws over a ladder. The genre's one instrument, but the filter tracks
-- an envelope that closes in 400 ms, so it is a stab rather than a chord — a
-- pad that sustained here would smear the pulse into mush.
stab = voice {
  params = {
    cutoff = { 300, 9000, 2600, "hz" },
    detune = { 0, 0.02, 0.008 },
  },
  graph = function(n)
    local osc = zero()
    for i = -2, 3 do
      osc = osc + saw(n.hz * (1 + n.detune * (i - 0.5))) * 0.12
    end
    local env = adsr(ms(14), ms(400), 0.34, secs(1.1))
    -- Resonance at 0.42 with the cutoff moving 3x per note is a *moving
    -- formant*: a peak sliding through the harmonics, which the ear reads as
    -- a vowel. That is the ladder filter's famous quack. It is a sound; it is
    -- not this sound. 0.16 and a much smaller sweep keep the warmth and lose
    -- the vowel.
    return (osc | n.cutoff * (1 + 0.9 * env) | dc(0.16)) >> moog()
        >> mul(env * n.velocity * lvl_stabs)
        >> shape("tanh", 1.3)
        >> chorus(0, 0.014, 0.006, 0.34)
  end,
}

-- The engine. A saw, a narrow square beside it, a sine for the fundamental,
-- a ladder, and a decay short enough that consecutive eighths separate
-- instead of running together. Everything upbeat here is in this one voice.
--
-- Nothing in it plays below the written note. A square an octave down put
-- fundamentals at 35 Hz, which is inaudible, expensive in headroom, and most
-- of why the first mix measured 10 dB heavy against the reference.
drive = voice {
  params = {
    cutoff = { 120, 3000, 620, "hz" },
    bite = { 1, 6, 2.4 },
  },
  graph = function(n)
    local env = adsr(ms(6), ms(150), 0.22, ms(90))
    local osc = saw(n.hz) * 0.75
              + pulse(n.hz, 0.42) * 0.3
              + sine(n.hz) * 0.28
    -- The same fault and worse, because it fired on every eighth note: a
    -- resonant peak sweeping four octaves, 4.7 times a second.
    return (osc | n.cutoff * (1 + 1.2 * env) | dc(0.22)) >> moog()
        >> mul(env * n.velocity * 0.26 * lvl_bass)
        >> shape("tanh", n.bite)
        >> dcblock()
        >> pan(0)
  end,
}

-- This was called `ride`, which was a lie: three `ring()` resonators struck
-- by an impulse, partials at 2.3, 3.4 and 5.2 kHz, decaying over most of a
-- second. A cymbal is dense inharmonic noise; three clean modes are a struck
-- bar. So it is a bell, and it is named one now.
--
-- Its fader defaults to zero. Three long, loud, pitched partials every few
-- bars is a strong candidate for the most attention-grabbing thing in the
-- piece, but that is a listening judgement and this is how it gets made.
bell = voice {
  graph = function(n)
    local modes = {
      ring(2270, secs(0.9)) * 0.5,
      ring(3410, secs(0.7)) * 0.35,
      ring(5180, secs(0.5)) * 0.2,
    }
    return impulse() >> mix(modes)
        >> mul(n.velocity * 0.28 * lvl_bell)
        >> dcblock()
        >> pan(n.pan)
  end,
}

-- The arp voice. It was a bright plucked saw and it drew the ear for four
-- separate reasons, worth listing because they are four different knobs and
-- "less bell" is not one of them: a 3 ms attack, a filter opening to 9 kHz,
-- panning past 0.5, and a 0.55 delay send.
--
-- It is a wooden mallet now — sine partials instead of a saw, an 18 ms swell
-- in front of it, and a filter that stays under 2 kHz. The 2.76 ratio is
-- deliberately not a whole number: struck wood is inharmonic, and that is
-- most of the difference between a marimba and an organ pipe.
mallet = voice {
  params = { cutoff = { 400, 6000, 1500, "hz" }, ring = { 0.08, 1.2, 0.42, "s" } },
  graph = function(n)
    local env = decay(n.ring) * (1 - decay(ms(18)))
    local tone = sine(n.hz) * 0.6
               + sine(n.hz * 2.76) * 0.16
               + sine(n.hz * 5.4) * 0.09
               + saw(n.hz) * 0.12
    return (tone | n.cutoff * (0.6 + 0.8 * env) | dc(0.4)) >> lowpass()
        >> mul(env * n.velocity * 0.5 * lvl_mallet)
        >> pan(n.pan)
  end,
}

-- New, and the reason the palette stops sounding like one instrument through
-- eight filters. This is the only voice here that sustains: everything else
-- is struck, plucked or stabbed, so a held chord underneath reads as a change
-- of section rather than as one more layer.
wash = voice {
  params = { cutoff = { 300, 5000, 1100, "hz" } },
  graph = function(n)
    local osc = soft_saw(n.hz) * 0.3
              + soft_saw(n.hz * 1.005) * 0.26
              + pulse(n.hz * 2, 0.3) * 0.07
    local swell = 1 + 0.22 * sine(0.09)
    return (osc | n.cutoff * swell | dc(0.5)) >> lowpass()
        >> mul(adsr(secs(1.4), secs(1.2), 0.8, secs(2.6)) * n.velocity * lvl_pad)
        >> chorus(2, 0.022, 0.009, 0.19)
  end,
}

-- The lead sings rather than plucks: slow attack, real vibrato, and the only
-- part in the piece allowed to hold a note across a bar line.
sing = voice {
  params = { bright = { 800, 6000, 2400, "hz" } },
  graph = function(n)
    local vibrato = 1 + 0.005 * sine(5.2) * window(secs(0.35), secs(12))
    local hz = n.hz * vibrato
    local osc = saw(hz) * 0.45
              + saw(hz * 1.006) * 0.4
              + sine(hz * 2) * 0.12
    local env = adsr(ms(120), secs(0.8), 0.72, secs(1.4))
    return (osc | n.bright * (1 + 1.4 * env) | dc(0.4)) >> lowpass()
        >> mul(env * n.velocity * 0.4 * lvl_lead)
        >> shape("soft", 1.15)
        >> chorus(1, 0.026, 0.011, 0.24)
        >> pan(n.pan)
  end,
}

-- Analogue kick: a pitch drop into a body, with the click left in this time.
-- `drift.eod` removed the transient because a transient interrupts; here the
-- transient is the beat, and 12 ms of noise is what makes it audible on a
-- laptop at all.
kick = voice {
  graph = function(n)
    local pitch = 55 + 85 * decay(ms(30))
    local body = (pitch >> sine()) * decay(ms(120)) * (1 - decay(ms(2)))
    local click = ((noise() | dc(2200) | dc(1.4)) >> bandpass())
                * decay(ms(10)) * 0.22
    return (body + click)
        >> mul(n.velocity * 0.6 * lvl_kick)
        >> shape("tanh", 1.5)
        >> dcblock()
        >> pan(0)
  end,
}

-- Gated snare, which in 1984 meant a huge plate with the tail chopped off
-- mid-swell. The gate is a `window`, so the length is a parameter of the
-- event rather than a property of the sound.
snare = voice {
  params = { gate = { 0.04, 0.5, 0.16, "s" } },
  graph = function(n)
    local crack = (noise() | dc(1800) | dc(1.1)) >> bandpass()
    local body = sine(190) * 0.4 + sine(315) * 0.24
    local hit = (crack + body) * decay(ms(60)) * (1 - decay(ms(2)))
    local tail = hit >> reverb(30, secs(1.9), 0.3)
    return (hit * 0.8 + tail * window(0, n.gate) * 0.9)
        >> mul(n.velocity * 0.5 * lvl_snare)
  end,
}

-- Band-passed rather than high-passed. A high-pass has no upper limit, so
-- every hat in the piece was contributing full-scale energy at 13 kHz, which
-- is most of what "harsh" means and none of what "bright" means.
hat = voice {
  params = { tone = { 2000, 10000, 5200, "hz" }, ring = { 0.01, 0.3, 0.05, "s" } },
  graph = function(n)
    return (noise() | dc(n.tone) | dc(0.9)) >> bandpass()
        >> mul(decay(n.ring) * (1 - decay(ms(1.5))) * n.velocity * 0.5 * lvl_hats)
        >> pan(n.pan)
  end,
}

-- The 7-bar accent. A tuned tom is the one percussion voice whose entrances
-- you notice, which is exactly what a layer at a prime length is for.
tom = voice {
  graph = function(n)
    local pitch = n.hz * (1 + 1.1 * decay(ms(70)))
    return (pitch >> sine()) * decay(ms(320)) * (1 - decay(ms(3)))
        >> mul(n.velocity * 0.5 * lvl_tom)
        >> shape("tanh", 1.2)
        >> dcblock()
        >> pan(n.pan)
  end,
}

---------------------------------------------------------------------- shape

-- i - VI - III - VII in C# minor as C#m9, Amaj9, E6, B7sus-ish, two bars
-- each. The top three voices barely move; only the bottom one walks.
chords = pattern("<[c#3,g#3,b3,d#4] [a2,g#3,b3,c#4] [e3,g#3,b3,c#4] [b2,f#3,a3,c#4]>")
      >> slow(2)

-- The sustained pad's own voicing, written out rather than derived from
-- `chords`. `octave(n)` would have been the obvious move and does not apply:
-- it adds to a *numeric* primary value, and a mini-notation note name is not
-- one. It is for chord/voicing-constructed data.
--
-- Three notes, sitting in the gap between the bass and the stabs, and moving
-- by at most a whole tone. A pad that doubles the stabs is not a pad, it is a
-- longer stab.
wash_chords = pattern("<[g#2,c#3,e3] [a2,c#3,e3] [g#2,b2,e3] [f#2,b2,d#3]>")
      >> slow(2)

-- Roots, straight eighths, for the whole eight bars. This is the pulse and it
-- does not vary, because a pulse that varies is a fill.
roots = pattern("<c#2*8 a1*8 e2*8 b1*8>") >> slow(2)

-- The 5-bar arp cell and the 7-bar lead cell, both C# minor pentatonic, so
-- both stay consonant wherever the 8-bar harmony has got to. Neither is
-- written against a particular chord — that is the point of the technique.
arp_cell = pattern([[<
  [c#4 ~ g#4 ~ b4 ~ g#4 ~]
  [~ f#4 ~ c#5 ~ b4 ~ ~]
  [e4 ~ b4 ~ c#5 ~ b4 ~]
  [~ ~ g#4 ~ f#4 ~ e4 ~]
  [c#5 ~ b4 ~ g#4 ~ ~ ~]
>]])

lead_cell = pattern([[<
  [~ ~ e5 ~]
  [~ ~ ~ ~]
  [c#5 ~ ~ b4]
  [~ ~ ~ ~]
  [~ g#4 ~ ~]
  [~ ~ ~ ~]
  [f#4 ~ e4 ~]
>]])

-- The slow movements, in bars. 11 and 13 divide neither the 8-bar harmony nor
-- any loop length above, so no parameter ever lines up with any phrase.
sweep  = scale(850, 2500, sine(1 / 11))
grit   = scale(1.9, 3.4, sine(1 / 13))
weight = scale(0.9, 1.0, sine(1 / 13))

-- The section gates. `slow` stretches a whole cycle, not a step, so eight
-- steps over `slow(32)` is four bars per step and a 32-bar cycle. Multiplied
-- into a velocity, a zero is an absent instrument rather than a quiet one,
-- because every graph here applies `n.velocity`.
--
-- These are patterns, not curves, which is the whole point: a curve has an
-- end and this does not.
local function blocks(spec)
  return pattern(spec) >> slow(32)
end

kick_blocks   = blocks("1 1 1 1 1 1 0 1")
snare_blocks  = blocks("0 1 1 1 1 1 0 1")
hat_blocks    = blocks("0 0 1 1 1 1 0 1")
stab_blocks   = blocks("1 1 0.4 0.4 1 1 1 0.45")
mallet_blocks = blocks("0 1 1 0 0 1 1 1")
bell_blocks   = blocks("0 0 0 0 1 1 1 1")
lead_blocks   = blocks("0 0 0 1 1 0 0 1")
wash_blocks   = blocks("1 1 0 0 1 0 1 0")

---------------------------------------------------------------------- score

------------------------------------------------------ 8 bars: the spine
-- The one layer with no section gate. If the bass goes, there is nothing to
-- hear the rest as early or late against.
play(drive, roots
      >> velocity(0.66 * weight)
      >> drive.cutoff(scale(460, 1500, sine(1 / 11)))
      >> drive.bite(grit)
      >> duck("1 0 1 0", 0.18))

play(stab, chords
      >> velocity(0.52 * weight * stab_blocks)
      >> stab.cutoff(sweep)
      >> pan("-0.4 0.4")
      >> to(hall, 0.5)
      >> duck("1 0 1 0", 0.13))

------------------------------------------------------ 4 bars: the beat
play(kick, pattern("<[bd ~ ~ ~ bd ~ ~ ~] [bd ~ ~ ~ bd ~ ~ ~] [bd ~ ~ ~ bd ~ ~ ~] [bd ~ ~ bd bd ~ ~ [~ bd]]>")
      >> velocity(0.78 * weight * kick_blocks))

play(snare, pattern("<[~ ~ x ~] [~ ~ x ~] [~ ~ x ~] [~ ~ x [~ x]]>")
      >> velocity(0.85 * snare_blocks)
      >> snare.gate("0.14 0.22")
      >> to(hall, 0.3))

------------------------------------------------------ 3 bars: the offbeat
-- Offbeat only. Eighths on the beat would double the kick and the bass and
-- turn a pulse into a wall.
play(hat, pattern("<[~ x ~ x ~ x ~ x] [~ x ~ x ~ x ~ [x x]] [~ x ~ x ~ [x x] ~ x]>")
      -- The accent has to be a *signal*, not a second pattern: two event
      -- patterns cannot be multiplied without an explicit temporal join, and
      -- rightly so. `sine(2)` has a half-bar period, so at the offbeat
      -- eighths this piece actually plays it alternates between its extremes
      -- — the same two-value accent, expressed at a rate rather than as a
      -- list.
      >> velocity(scale(0.30, 0.46, sine(2)) * hat_blocks)
      >> hat.tone(scale(3000, 4800, sine(1 / 7)))
      >> hat.ring("0.04 0.03 0.05 0.03")
      >> pan("-0.3 0.35"))

------------------------------------------------------ 5 bars, offset 2
play(mallet, arp_cell
      >> shift(bars(2))
      >> velocity(0.5 * weight * mallet_blocks)
      >> mallet.cutoff(scale(900, 1900, sine(1 / 11)))
      >> mallet.ring("0.4 0.3 0.5 0.34")
      >> pan("-0.3 0.28 -0.18 0.34")
      >> to(echo, 0.28)
      >> to(hall, 0.4))

play(bell, pattern("<[~ ~ x ~ ~ ~ x ~] [~ ~ x ~ ~ ~ ~ ~] [~ ~ x ~ ~ x ~ ~] ~ [~ ~ x ~ ~ ~ x [x ~]]>")
      >> shift(bars(2))
      >> velocity(0.55 * bell_blocks)
      >> pan("0.45 -0.4"))

------------------------------------------------------ 8 bars: the sustain
-- Same chords as the stabs, an octave apart in register and a world apart in
-- envelope. In the sections where the stabs drop to 0.4 this is what is left
-- holding the harmony, which is what makes those four bars read as a
-- different place rather than as a quieter version of the same one.
play(wash, wash_chords
      >> velocity(0.2 * weight * wash_blocks)
      >> wash.cutoff(scale(800, 1800, sine(1 / 13)))
      >> pan("0.35 -0.35")
      >> to(hall, 0.8))

------------------------------------------------------ 7 bars
play(sing, lead_cell
      >> velocity(0.66 * weight * lead_blocks)
      >> sing.bright(scale(1300, 2900, sine(1 / 11)))
      >> pan(scale(-0.25, 0.25, sine(1 / 13)))
      >> to(echo, 0.4)
      >> to(hall, 0.7))

play(tom, pattern("<~ [~ ~ ~ [g#2 ~]] ~ [~ ~ c#3 ~] ~ ~ [~ [g#2 c#3] ~ ~]>")
      >> shift(bars(3))
      >> velocity(0.55)
      >> pan("-0.4 0.4")
      >> to(hall, 0.45))

-- Slightly hot into a fast limiter, which is what the decade sounded like.
-- `width` is well above unity because the reference for this piece measures
-- about 6 dB of side against mid and the parts alone come nowhere near —
-- hard-panned material is wide, but a mix whose loudest two elements are a
-- mono bass and a mono kick is not, and those two set the denominator.
master(
    highpass(46)
    >> width(3.2)
    >> lowpass(10000, 0.5)
    >> shape("soft", 1.06)
    >> limiter(0.004, 0.18)
    >> mul(1.35)
)
